  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 350 PM EDT TUE SEP 1 2026\n\nAs the long term period opens, an anomalously strong 500 mb ridge\nwill still be located over the forecast area. By Friday, a trough\nover the Great Lakes region associated with a closed upper level \nlow over Quebec will begin to dig farther south, with this ridge \nbecoming temporarily less amplified as the ridge axis retrogrades \nwestward. Weather on Thursday and Friday will still be hot, owing \nto continued influence from this ridge; however, Friday will be \nnoticeably cooler than Thursday as the forecast area finds itself \non the periphery of this ridging rather than being directly \nbeneath it. In terms of sensible weather impacts, there is high \nconfidence that temperatures will be firmly above average; thus, \nthe main forecast question during this period is just how hot it \nwill get. Deterministic guidance is generally split into two camps\nin this regard: either dew points are lower with a higher air \ntemperature, or dewpoints are higher with a lower air temperature.\nThese drier solutions have proven unrepresentative in this \npattern thus far, largely because of unrealistically deep mixing; \ngiven this bias, it seems more likely that a cooler and higher \ndewpoint solution would come to fruition on Thursday and Friday. \nIn support of this, surface high pressure just south of the \nforecast area in Tennessee should promote westerly to \nsouthwesterly winds across the forecast area, maintaining these \nhigher dewpoints. Accordingly, MaxTs were nudged down Thursday and\nFriday, with highs in the low to mid 90s on Thursday and a few \ndegrees cooler on Friday. Meanwhile, dewpoints were largely \nmaintained, expected to be generally in the upper 60s to lower 70s\nacross the region. This will keep apparent temperatures just \nbelow heat advisory criteria, but triple digit heat indices are \nstill expected. Overnight Thursday, relief will be relatively \nlimited; clear skies and high pressure will allow for a modest \nridge-valley split, but low temperatures will still generally fall\ninto the low 70s on ridges and the upper 60s in valleys (where \ntypical fog formation is expected). \n\nBy the weekend, the base of the upper level trough will be to our\nsoutheast, with amplifying ridging west of the forecast area over\nthe Great Plains. This pattern will place eastern Kentucky in a \nnorthwest flow regime aloft. More appreciable PoPs look to return \nto the forecast as early as Friday as ridging breaks down and a \nweak cold front approaches. At least a slight chance of showers \nand storms is in the forecast through Saturday evening, in \nconjunction with this front and the potential for multiple \nshortwaves embedded within this northwesterly flow. As is typical \nin these patterns, model spread and uncertainty are high, \nparticularly in regard to the timing of these features; \naccordingly, NBM PoPs are quite broad brushed for now, \nrepresenting the averaging of numerous different timing solutions.\nThus, these PoPs will bear some refining later. Thankfully, this \npattern will also allow temperatures to return closer to normal, \nwith highs in the mid to upper 80s expected in the forecast area \nby Sunday afternoon. Ridging also looks to build back toward our \narea near the end of the period, which should mark an end to this \nwetter pattern.",
